A||||4||Wannabe||'88 Caliper Prone to Siezing?||||1||
Z||000000||Wannabe||08-28-2001||12:40 PM||||It seems somebody mentioned this before, but after doing a search, I couldn'd find anything. After spending another weekend at the track though, I'm convinced this is a problem. A complete brake job, short of a caliper rebuild, before heading out on Friday produced excellent braking performance. By the end of the day though, the pedal had already gone long and braking was eratic. An inspection revealed the front calipers were both siezed. Another cleaning and new lubricant restored the perfomance. After two runs the next day, the left front was seized again, and so it went all weekend. This is ridiculous. It seems the aluminum bores are poorly matched to the plated steel sliders. It might be possible to slightly enlarge the bores and press in bronze bushings - this would definitely compromise the seating of those ridiculously large boots (another part of the problem) and may even reduce the strength of the caliper mounting, but probably not enough to cause trouble. Anyone else have some experience with this problem?||||reg||1
Z||000001||rodmcneill||08-28-2001||01:22 PM||||Theogre mentioned that the lube that you use for the slider bolts has to be a brake specific grease not petrolium (sp) based. He said that regular grease can swell the seals and bind up the sliders.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Wannabe||08-28-2001||02:20 PM||||Of course. I'm using a high temp silicon lubricant for the sliders. I will check the cave for more info.||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||08-28-2001||04:51 PM||||it was probally me, I've had two caliper siezures on both of my 88s.  I just replaced the damn things. (this works- should get another 50-100k out of new hardware)<P>I think they're junk, but others disagree.<P>I'm working on some very nice 4-piston total brake replacements.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000004||theogre||08-28-2001||07:16 PM||||there's not much 88 specific in my cave...<P>take them off clean them completely and carefully inspect the slide holes. make sure there's no uneven wear or corrosion built up in them. I don't think those calipers have as much internal clearance in the slide holes as the older ones. (Have to be tighter to use that type slide.)<P>If there's any scale stuck in there it's got to come out. The problem is you don't want to increase clearance or you'll end up with a sloppy caliper and uneven pad loading.||||reg||1
